statement.p:
Consider the vectors
\vec{v_1} = \twovec{2}{1}, \vec{v_2} = \twovec{1}{2}
in \real^2, which are shown in .

li.p:
We note that
\left[\begin{array}{rrr} 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp -1 \amp -1 \end{array}\right] \sim \left[\begin{array}{rrr} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{array}\right]\text{.}
Since there is a pivot position in every row, the span of the vectors is \real^3. Since there is a pivot position in every column, the vectors are linearly independent. Consequently, this set of vectors forms a basis for \real^3.

statement.p:
Notice that the vectors
\vec{e_1} = \threevec{1}{0}{0}, \vec{e_2} = \threevec{0}{1}{0}, \vec{e_3} = \threevec{0}{0}{1}
form the columns of the 3\times3 identity matrix, which implies that this set forms a basis for \real^3. More generally, the set of vectors \vec{e_1},\vec{e_2},\ldots,\vec{e_m} forms a basis for \real^m, which we call the standard basis for \real^m. basis, standard

li.p:
If \vec{x}=\twovec{c_1}{c_2}, we have
\vec{x}=c_1\vec{v_1}+c_2\vec{v_2} = \left[\begin{array}{rr} 3 \amp 2 \\ -2 \amp 1 \\ \end{array}\right]\twovec{c_1}{c_2} = A \coords{\vec{x}}{\bcal}\text{.}
This matrix A, whose columns are the vectors \vec{v_1} and \vec{v_2}, has a pivot position in every row and every column because the vectors form a basis. It is, therefore, row equivalent to the identity matrix and hence invertible.

 If we define B to be the matrix whose columns
	  are \twovec{1}{2} and \twovec{-2}{1}, then

	    \coords{\vec{x}}{\bcal} = A^{-1}\vec{x} =
	    A^{-1}B\coords{\vec{x}}{\ccal}\text{.} 

	  Therefore,
	  C=A^{-1}B = \left[\begin{array}{rr}
	  -\frac{3}{7} \amp -\frac{4}{7} \\
	  \frac{8}{7} \amp -\frac{1}{7}
	  \end{array}\right]\text{.}

subsection.p:
This activity demonstrates how we can efficiently convert
   between coordinate systems defined by different bases.  Let's
   consider a basis \bcal = \{\vec{v_1},\vec{v_2},\ldots,\vec{v_m}\}
   and a vector \vec{x}.  We know that

     \begin{aligned}
     \vec{x} \amp {}={} c_1\vec{v_1} +c_2\vec{v_2}+\ldots+c_m\vec{v_m} \\
     \\
     \amp {}={}
     \left[\begin{array}{rrrr}
     \vec{v_1} \amp \vec{v_2} \amp \cdots \amp \vec{v_m}
     \end{array}\right]
     \cfourvec{c_1}{c_2}{\vdots}{c_m} \\
     \\
     \amp {}={}
     \left[\begin{array}{rrrr}
     \vec{v_1} \amp \vec{v_2} \amp \cdots \amp \vec{v_m}
     \end{array}\right]
     \coords{\vec{x}}{\bcal}\text{.}
     \end{aligned}

   If we use P_{\bcal} to denote the matrix whose columns are
   the basis vectors, then we find that

     \vec{x} = P_{\bcal}\coords{\vec{x}}{\bcal}

   where P_{\bcal} = \left[\begin{array}{rrrr}
   \vec{v_1} \amp \vec{v_2} \amp \cdots \amp \vec{v_m}
   \end{array}\right].  This means that the matrix
   P_{\bcal} converts coordinates in the basis \bcal
   into standard coordinates.

Since the columns of P_{\bcal} are the basis vectors
   \vec{v_1},\vec{v_2},\ldots,\vec{v_m}, we know that P_{\bcal}
   \sim I_m, and P_{\bcal} is therefore invertible.
   Since we have

     \vec{x} = P_{\bcal}\coords{\vec{x}}{\bcal}
   ,
   we must also have

     P_{\bcal}^{-1}\vec{x} = \coords{\vec{x}}{\bcal}
   .

statement.p:
If \bcal is a basis and P_{\bcal} the matrix
whose columns are the basis vectors, then

  \begin{aligned}
  \vec{x} \amp = P_{\bcal}\coords{\vec{x}}{\bcal}, \\ \\
  \coords{\vec{x}}{\bcal} \amp = P_{\bcal}^{-1}\vec{x}.
  \end{aligned}

li.p:
 We compute that
	\coords{\vec{x}}{\bcal}=P_{\bcal}^{-1}\vec{x} =
	\left[\begin{array}{c}
	-9 \\ 4 \\ -15 \\ -145 \\ -10 \\ 200
	\end{array}\right]\text{.}
